                        <exData contentType="text/html">
                           <body xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml" xmlns:xfa="http://www.xfa.org/schema/xfa-data/1.0/"><p style="font-size:14pt;font-weight:bold;letter-spacing:0in">IMPORTANT NOTE:<span style="font-size:10pt;font-weight:normal"><br/>The instructions below apply to the parent's spouse. Notify the financial aid office if the parent's spouse filed separate IRS income tax returns for 2022 or had a change in marital status after December 31, 2022. If you<span style="xfa-spacerun:yes"> </span></span><span style="font-size:10pt;font-weight:normal;letter-spacing:0em">successfully</span><span style="font-size:10pt;font-weight:normal"> consented and submitted your correction, you will no longer have to submit tax return transcripts. Please follow<span style="xfa-spacerun:yes"> </span></span><span style="font-size:10pt;text-decoration:underline">two business weeks</span><span style="font-size:10pt;font-weight:normal"> for our office to download these corrections. Shortly after we will remove the Parent Tax Filing Statement from your task list.</span></p><p style="font-weight:bold;letter-spacing:0in">Please Note:<span style="xfa-spacerun:yes"> </span><span style="font-weight:normal">Not everyone can use the IRS DDX. If you cannot use the IRS DDX, you may have to request the tax return scripts.<br/></span><span style="font-size:14pt"><br/>INSTRUCTIONS:</span><span style="font-weight:normal"><br/>As part of federal student aid eligibility, parent will be required to consent and approve sharing and importing income and tax information from the IRS to the FAFSA form, even if the attempt to obtain or use such data is ineffective. In other words, if the parent and spouse filed separate 2022 IRS income tax returns, both must provide consent and approval to share and import income and tax information from the IRS. In most cases, no further documentation is needed to verify 2022 income information that was transferred into the student's FAFSA using income and tax information directly from the IRS.<br/></span><br/></p></body>
